---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const canonical = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href;

const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Organization',
  '@id': `${new URL('#org', Astro.site).href}`,
  name: 'Example',
  url: canonical,
  logo: new URL('/social/logo.svg', Astro.site).href,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About this documentation set — Example Docs"
  description="Who publishes Example Docs, how the pages are produced, and what the site deliberately leaves out."
  canonical={canonical}
  image="https://example.com/social/about.png"
  imageAlt="A single page of documentation shown beside the markdown file it was built from."
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <h1>About this documentation set</h1>
  <p>
    Example Docs exists to describe its own build. Each page is a markdown file in
    one collection, rendered by one layout, written to disk by one command. There is
    no editor, no database and no request-time work of any kind.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pages carry no advertising, no comment form and no reader accounts, so a
    reader arriving from anywhere sees the same bytes. That is a deliberate limit
    rather than an omission: the narrower the site, the easier it is to say exactly
    what each published file contains.
  </p>
  <p>
    Corrections belong in the markdown file the page was built from. The entry ids
    under <a href="/docs/routing">Routing</a> name the files directly, so a reader
    who spots an error can find the source without searching for it.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
